combinatorial scenarios. The thermal comfort profiles were selected to be distributed across a range of preferred indoor temperatures to show the impact of diversity in individual differences. Moreover, we ac-counted for different numbers and combinations of occupants, two thermal zone configurations, three control strategies, and three climate conditions. In these evaluations, we looked at the histogram of energy use across different combinatorial realizations, leading to a compre-hensive evaluation of comfort-driven control.
The results showed that the occupants’ thermal comfort character-istics and the number of occupants per thermal zone are the most im-pactful parameters in shaping the energy use distributions and de-termining the energy saving potentials of comfort-driven control. In other words, if the average of preferred temperatures by occupants in a building lies above the standard setpoints, the probability of energy saving increases and vice versa. However, regardless of the average preferred temperature by groups of occupants, diversity in personal differences could create energy saving opportunities in a building. Our analyses over a wide spectrum of occupants’ characteristics show an average estimate for cumulative probability of energy saving cases in a range between 3.5% and 80.3% for a single thermal zone and in a range between 16.9% and 95.2% for multiple thermal zones. For the best performing control strategy, the lower-bound values improve and these ranges will be 29.2%−80.3% and 57.4%−95.2%, respectively. The average (±standrad deviation) energy saving potentials from these cases were between −3.7 ± 4.8% and 5.3 ± 5.6% for a single zone, and between −3.1 ± 4.9% and 9.1 ± 5.1% for multiple zones. For the best performing control strategy, the lower-bound average energy savings were −0.7% and −0.3%, respectively.
Analyses on energy efficiency (i.e., the use of energy for improving thermal comfort) showed that as the number of occupants per multi-occupancy zones increases, the opportunities for energy efficiency im-provement by using personal comfort profiles decreases. The use of comfort-driven control showed to have an upper-bound average of 21% to 11% of improvements in energy efficiency when a thermal zone is shared by two to six occupants, respectively. By having more than six occupants per zone, the benefits of using personal comfort diminish although there are still potentials for improved energy performance. In those cases, the performance from the integration of personal comfort models converges to that of conventional generalized models. In addi-tion, control strategies were proven to be effective in driving the energy efficiency of operations. Specifically, the thermal-comfort-sensitivity-based approach showed to result in the best performance in generating collectively comfortable conditions and the majority rule approach was shown to be more energy efficient than the other strategies. Among the future directions of this study are the investigation of energy efficiency potentials at the intersection of occupancy and comfort modalities, as well as investigating the impact of temperature distributions through more advanced simulations or field studies, where the temperature distributions across a thermal zone could be affected by different con-textual variables and result in varied occupant experiences.